# kiosk-linux/configs/busybox-net.fragment
# Ensures BusyBox's udhcpc DHCP client applet is built in. Buildroot's default
# BusyBox config already enables this in most releases, but this fragment
# makes it an explicit, non-negotiable requirement for this project rather
# than an implicit default that could silently regress.
CONFIG_UDHCPC=y
# nc (netcat) backs S98kiosk-net-marker's reachability probe: a TCP connect to
# rmpgutah.us:443 proves DNS + routing + outbound TCP without needing TLS
# (BusyBox wget has none) or port-80 egress (a real 2026-07-24 run showed
# plain-HTTP fetches to third-party hosts timing out on a network where the
# browser's HTTPS load to the same target worked fine).
CONFIG_NC=y
# CONFIG_NC alone builds nc in its MINIMAL form — usage is literally
# "nc [IPADDR PORT]" with NO option flags, so `nc -w 5 -z host 443` printed a
# usage message and exited non-zero. That produced a false KIOSK_LINUX_NET_FAILED
# on boots where the browser demonstrably loaded the site over HTTPS (confirmed
# by running both probe forms interactively in the guest, 2026-07-24; DNS
# resolved fine in the same run). These two options add what the probe needs:
#   NC_EXTRA       -> -w SEC (connection timeout, so a dead network cannot hang boot)
#   NC_110_COMPAT  -> -z     (connect-and-close with a meaningful exit code)
# Without -z the minimal form would block waiting for server data that never
# arrives on a bare TCP connect to 443, and the timeout kill would look like a
# failure even on success.
CONFIG_NC_EXTRA=y
CONFIG_NC_110_COMPAT=y

# ntpd — the client half only. Added 2026-07-25 after the completeness audit
# found the image had no way to set its clock at all: whatever the RTC said at
# power-on stood for the rest of the shift, with no correction ever.
#
# That is not a cosmetic problem on this terminal. TLS certificate validity and
# JWT exp/nbf are both checked against system time, so a drifted clock takes
# rmpgutah.us offline and logs officers out — and reports itself as neither.
# See the timezone block in configs/desktop.fragment for the display half.
#
# FEATURE_NTPD_SERVER is deliberately left off: a patrol terminal is a time
# CLIENT, and serving NTP would be an unnecessary listening socket on a machine
# that rides public networks. FEATURE_NTPD_CONF (parse /etc/ntp.conf) is also
# off — the servers are passed on the command line by S98kiosk-timesync, which
# keeps the configuration in one reviewable place instead of two.
CONFIG_NTPD=y
# CONFIG_FEATURE_NTPD_SERVER is not set
# CONFIG_FEATURE_NTPD_CONF is not set
